#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087513 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d9d8d4d2-fef7-5ada-8691-3f504cb3cf97" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Horizontal Asymptote When the Degrees of the Numerator and Denominator are Equal"}] :question/id #uuid "eb0b899e-af3a-587c-96ae-f1da9e0929dd" :question/math-academy-id "e-5847" :question/problem "Determine the horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x) = \\dfrac{4x^3-x}{3x^3+24}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to\\infty.$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is $x^3.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by $x^3,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{4{x}^{3}-x}{3{x}^{3}+24} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{x}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{3{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{24}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(4-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}{(3+\\frac{24}{{x}^{3}})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\dfrac{1}{x^2}$ and $\\dfrac{24}{x^3}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to\\infty,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(4-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}{(3+\\frac{24}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & \\to \\frac{4-0}{3+0} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the horizontal asymptote is $y = \\dfrac{4}{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087514 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6f75252e-bf7d-59ed-bb16-6a2b65a97863" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Horizontal Asymptote When the Degree of the Denominator is Greater"}] :question/id #uuid "0d53819b-ca24-549d-98e2-a9b78b49c080" :question/math-academy-id "e-5849" :question/problem "Determine the horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x) = \\dfrac{3x^2+ 2x}{6x^4-1}$." :question/worked-solution "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to\\infty.$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is $x^4.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by $x^4,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}+2x}{6{x}^{4}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{{x}^{4}}+\\frac{2x}{{x}^{4}})}{(\\frac{6{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}})}{(6-\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms  $\\dfrac{3}{x^2}, \\dfrac{2}{x^3},$ and $\\dfrac{1}{x^4}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to\\infty,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}})}{(6-\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & \\to \\frac{0+0}{6-0} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the horizontal asymptote is $y = 0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087515 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "653503b2-4631-561e-af5e-847bcbdfe8e2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Horizontal Asymptote When the Degree of the Numerator is Greater"}] :question/id #uuid "ea2a97a7-fce8-5b4a-a269-57e0ae7984d8" :question/math-academy-id "e-5851" :question/problem "Determine the horizontal asymptote of $f(x) = \\dfrac{2x^3}{x^2-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to\\infty.$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is $x^2.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by $x^2,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{(1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the term $\\dfrac{1}{x^2}$ becomes very small. Therefore, as $x\\to\\infty,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2x}{(1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{2x}{1-0} \\\\  & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is not a constant, which means that $y = f(x)$ does not have a horizontal asymptote."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339719 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "653503b2-4631-561e-af5e-847bcbdfe8e2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Horizontal Asymptote When the Degree of the Numerator is Greater"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e6d2b9b-4fc5-55f5-b0b8-4d24a4f93df6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No horizontal asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339721} {:answer/id #uuid "d58c7b64-8918-5e5e-aec6-48bf6fa9c2b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339722} {:answer/id #uuid "1bfb1810-7ea8-57b7-9b69-530c586fb05f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339723} {:answer/id #uuid "98ec2de6-efe2-5639-97e3-70eed9db85f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339724} {:answer/id #uuid "cb1d8512-c4a3-5f5f-a68b-5671b307a8ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339725}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2e6d2b9b-4fc5-55f5-b0b8-4d24a4f93df6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No horizontal asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339721} :answer-field/id #uuid "026eb333-7fe3-509a-a7be-815358835cf0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339720}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "98127c5e-809c-59c2-bd79-ffd78a0e8150" :question/math-academy-id "q-113178" :question/problem "Determine the horizontal asymptote of $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{4}-3x+2}{{x}^{2}+1}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is ${x}^{2}.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by ${x}^{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{4}-3x+2}{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{3x}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{2}-\\frac{3}{x}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}})}{(1+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\frac{3}{x},$ $\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}$ and $\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{({x}^{2}-\\frac{3}{x}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}})}{(1+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{{x}^{2}-0+0}{1+0} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is not a constant, which means that $y=f(x)$ does not have a horizontal asymptote."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339726 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6f75252e-bf7d-59ed-bb16-6a2b65a97863" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Horizontal Asymptote When the Degree of the Denominator is Greater"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "65e86732-e23d-595c-bbfc-1848f151b0df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339728} {:answer/id #uuid "c8d1141b-25e5-56ea-b417-0514bd6b08d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339729}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c8d1141b-25e5-56ea-b417-0514bd6b08d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339729} :answer-field/id #uuid "7cee78a3-8515-5442-ab24-23c06e0410b1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339727}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "beaf7cea-b83a-5727-986d-b09c501e0dda" :question/math-academy-id "q-269988" :question/problem "The horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x)=\\frac{7{x}^{3}+2x-5}{{x}^{2}(2{x}^{2}+3)}$ is $y=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The given function can be rewritten as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{7{x}^{3}+2x-5}{2{x}^{4}+3{x}^{2}}\n$$\n\nTo find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is ${x}^{4}.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by ${x}^{4},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{7{x}^{3}+2x-5}{2{x}^{4}+3{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{7{x}^{3}}{{x}^{4}}+\\frac{2x}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{5}{{x}^{4}})}{(\\frac{2{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}}+\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{7}{x}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{5}{{x}^{4}})}{(2+\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\frac{7}{x},$ $\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}},$ $\\frac{5}{{x}^{4}},$ and $\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(\\frac{7}{x}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{5}{{x}^{4}})}{(2+\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & \\to \\frac{0+0-0}{2+0} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the horizontal asymptote is $y=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339730 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d9d8d4d2-fef7-5ada-8691-3f504cb3cf97" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Horizontal Asymptote When the Degrees of the Numerator and Denominator are Equal"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e50a09ba-5999-52cc-9af4-c94ee64d2e85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339732} {:answer/id #uuid "24af47c0-2640-5c41-b998-cd09c77a329f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339733}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e50a09ba-5999-52cc-9af4-c94ee64d2e85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339732} :answer-field/id #uuid "c945c694-1169-5142-8b42-5f4a3989b151" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339731}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ce3539b4-30f1-5ce1-a505-47ade32dd7c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-277944" :question/problem "The horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}-8{x}^{3}}{2{x}^{3}-5x}$ is $y=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIn this case, the dominant term in the denominator is ${x}^{3}.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by ${x}^{3},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-8{x}^{3}}{2{x}^{3}-5x} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{8{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{5x}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{x}-8)}{(2-\\frac{5}{{x}^{2}})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\frac{1}{x}$ and $\\frac{5}{{x}^{2}}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{x}-8)}{(2-\\frac{5}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & \\to \\frac{0-8}{2+0} \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the horizontal asymptote is $y=-4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339734 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d9d8d4d2-fef7-5ada-8691-3f504cb3cf97" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Horizontal Asymptote When the Degrees of the Numerator and Denominator are Equal"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5510eba2-a148-577b-b9dd-1062d7e1e47c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339736} {:answer/id #uuid "7d6ca3bf-89f0-5f47-90d2-b209a39b37c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339737}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5510eba2-a148-577b-b9dd-1062d7e1e47c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339736} :answer-field/id #uuid "a035e5ab-32a6-5d8c-ac97-ec29000584e4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339735}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9bd55036-b9b9-5eb4-a252-ea929e1a8c76" :question/math-academy-id "q-277946" :question/problem "The horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x)=\\frac{4{x}^{3}-5{x}^{2}+3}{2-{x}^{3}}$ is $y=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIn this case, the dominant term in the denominator is ${x}^{3}.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by ${x}^{3},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{4{x}^{3}-5{x}^{2}+3}{2-{x}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(4-\\frac{5}{x}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}-1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\frac{5}{x},$ $\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}},$ and $\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(4-\\frac{5}{x}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}-1)} \\\\  & \\to \\frac{4-0+0}{0-1} \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the horizontal asymptote is $y=-4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339738 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d9d8d4d2-fef7-5ada-8691-3f504cb3cf97" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Horizontal Asymptote When the Degrees of the Numerator and Denominator are Equal"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ed80573-88a0-54b0-8f21-2cf9ed1f47ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339740}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1ed80573-88a0-54b0-8f21-2cf9ed1f47ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339740} :answer-field/id #uuid "201eb6f5-fda9-5cd3-a7ee-5e7283a76cd9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339739}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e2c3f2cc-7f24-5e07-8519-d1c4475ba3bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-277950" :question/problem "The horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x)=\\frac{8{x}^{4}+6x}{({x}^{2}-2x)(1-2{x}^{2})}$ is $y=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The given function can be rewritten as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{8{x}^{4}-6x}{-2{x}^{4}+4{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-2x}\n$$\n\nTo find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is ${x}^{4}.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by ${x}^{4},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{8{x}^{4}-6x}{-2{x}^{4}+4{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-2x} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{8{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{6x}{{x}^{4}})}{(-\\frac{2{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}}+\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{{x}^{4}}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{2x}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(8-\\frac{6}{{x}^{3}})}{(-2+\\frac{4}{x}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\frac{6}{{x}^{3}},$ $\\frac{4}{x},$ $\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}},$ and $\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(8-\\frac{6}{{x}^{3}})}{(-2+\\frac{4}{x}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & \\to \\frac{8-0}{-2+0+0-0} \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the horizontal asymptote is $y=-4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339741 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "653503b2-4631-561e-af5e-847bcbdfe8e2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Horizontal Asymptote When the Degree of the Numerator is Greater"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f7bdf8b-0782-5c1f-ab53-4f6ca30d5393" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339743} {:answer/id #uuid "68a1f178-1a55-5d78-945a-f72ae95a88d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No horizontal asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339744} {:answer/id #uuid "a94108af-99f3-5baf-96c1-18259cd30300" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339745} {:answer/id #uuid "5ec79457-133e-5aa0-bdb1-0a4606a5fe44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339746} {:answer/id #uuid "5a95b8fe-da81-5d82-838f-4bf246fa322e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339747}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "68a1f178-1a55-5d78-945a-f72ae95a88d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No horizontal asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339744} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d6b794f-a08f-5ebb-a767-5d926c975bec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339742}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6e310bd5-107e-5272-9b9a-310ea9e9c58c" :question/math-academy-id "q-4003" :question/problem "Determine the horizontal asymptote of $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{3}+2x+1}{{x}^{2}-x-12}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is ${x}^{2}.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by ${x}^{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{3}+2x+1}{{x}^{2}-x-12} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{12}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+\\frac{2}{x}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}{(1-\\frac{1}{x}-\\frac{12}{{x}^{2}})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\frac{2}{x},\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}},\\frac{1}{x}$ and $\\frac{12}{{x}^{2}}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(x+\\frac{2}{x}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}{(1-\\frac{1}{x}-\\frac{12}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{x+0+0}{1-0-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{1} \\\\  & =x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is not a constant, which means that $y=f(x)$ does not have a horizontal asymptote."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339748 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6f75252e-bf7d-59ed-bb16-6a2b65a97863" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Horizontal Asymptote When the Degree of the Denominator is Greater"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e1bdf93f-8991-5338-97ac-b6b8bd2c1303" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339750} {:answer/id #uuid "8a0163bc-cc15-5d05-ab75-079db408fe0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No horizontal asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339751} {:answer/id #uuid "8a7e8a08-6276-51dc-8410-e134a6efb944" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339752} {:answer/id #uuid "a7a70a26-11ba-5460-b905-eae3cfb8b247" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339753} {:answer/id #uuid "0b515983-8e73-5142-a419-36d643213b84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339754}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a7a70a26-11ba-5460-b905-eae3cfb8b247" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339753} :answer-field/id #uuid "d1ce21a4-9257-5363-8037-d7ca096467f3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339749}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9d9688b7-9ace-5539-892f-eb62fedb06dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-4637" :question/problem "Determine the horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x)=\\frac{3x-{x}^{2}}{5{x}^{4}-4}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is ${x}^{4}.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by ${x}^{4},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{3x-{x}^{2}}{5{x}^{4}-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3x}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{4}})}{(\\frac{5{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{4}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}{(5-\\frac{4}{{x}^{4}})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}},\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}$ and $\\frac{4}{{x}^{4}}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}{(5-\\frac{4}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & \\to \\frac{0-0}{5-0} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the horizontal asymptote is $y=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339755 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6f75252e-bf7d-59ed-bb16-6a2b65a97863" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Horizontal Asymptote When the Degree of the Denominator is Greater"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f21b18e-0c6b-5af6-8d44-0cfb5cfe08b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No horizontal asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339757} {:answer/id #uuid "f9cc8590-f704-554f-b54d-c15637d1693a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339758} {:answer/id #uuid "f0f5bf6b-a002-59fe-9d72-9d148b41a070" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339759} {:answer/id #uuid "abf238ed-1aa2-5ac8-8d73-44047bc40ff0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339760} {:answer/id #uuid "32fcd702-974e-5759-8bb5-f286db51bd66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339761}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f9cc8590-f704-554f-b54d-c15637d1693a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339758} :answer-field/id #uuid "8c25582f-b139-54e4-94ef-606d9e65aea0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339756}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ee2ffb43-85ed-5cc3-a4b8-d0427403c68f" :question/math-academy-id "q-4699" :question/problem "Determine the horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+2x-3}{{x}^{3}-x}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is ${x}^{3}.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by ${x}^{3},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}+2x-3}{{x}^{3}-x} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{2x}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{x}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}})}{(1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\frac{1}{x},\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}},\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}}$ and $\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}})}{(1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & \\to \\frac{0+0-0}{1-0} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the horizontal asymptote is $y=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088130 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d9d8d4d2-fef7-5ada-8691-3f504cb3cf97" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Horizontal Asymptote When the Degrees of the Numerator and Denominator are Equal"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9e71fa98-4e28-508a-b90f-e758426900b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085712} {:answer/id #uuid "c838eeb5-25e4-53b7-8579-13e7828fbf15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No horizontal asymptote exist" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085713} {:answer/id #uuid "d350d3ea-d732-56c1-9538-ce5cfe427a7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085714} {:answer/id #uuid "db83563c-65ea-5ecd-8351-595ec01a9100" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085715} {:answer/id #uuid "408af430-0e0a-5d83-abe6-8e26898c0497" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085716}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d350d3ea-d732-56c1-9538-ce5cfe427a7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085714} :answer-field/id #uuid "3de71cb3-404d-5dc9-b9cb-d8d64a594609" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085711}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2c808076-b153-5a08-a330-1381ba18772e" :question/math-academy-id "q-4700" :question/problem "Determine the horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x)=\\frac{2x({x}^{3}+1)}{3{x}^{4}-7}.$" :question/worked-solution "The given function can be rewritten as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{2{x}^{4}+2x}{3{x}^{4}-7}\n$$\n\nTo find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is ${x}^{4}.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by ${x}^{4},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2{x}^{4}+2x}{3{x}^{4}-7} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{2{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}}+\\frac{2x}{{x}^{4}})}{(\\frac{3{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{7}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(2+\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}})}{(3-\\frac{7}{{x}^{4}})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}$ and $\\frac{7}{{x}^{4}}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(2+\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}})}{(3-\\frac{7}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & \\to \\frac{2+0}{3-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the horizontal asymptote is $y=\\frac{2}{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339762 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d9d8d4d2-fef7-5ada-8691-3f504cb3cf97" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Horizontal Asymptote When the Degrees of the Numerator and Denominator are Equal"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c1e37e1f-b27e-5cdd-84a7-4cffe2090c39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339764} {:answer/id #uuid "09df62fa-6823-54c4-a245-bfb6fd9a4ead" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No horizontal asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339765} {:answer/id #uuid "09c9fd94-ed39-50fc-ae5c-95b8cf29e57f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339766} {:answer/id #uuid "2182c1cf-1af9-50b0-bb5b-7a10057cd822" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339767} {:answer/id #uuid "84e99b9a-1e5f-54d7-b601-e7075db883a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339768}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2182c1cf-1af9-50b0-bb5b-7a10057cd822" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339767} :answer-field/id #uuid "92ae7366-19e6-5142-a520-27c9d218a1d6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339763}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fb0a0ff9-89bd-5ff4-9372-c1be8a3ccf21" :question/math-academy-id "q-6931" :question/problem "Determine the horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x)=\\frac{4{x}^{2}+6x+1}{1-2{x}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIn this case, the dominant term in the denominator is ${x}^{2}.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by ${x}^{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{4{x}^{2}+6x+1}{1-2{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{4{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{6x}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(4+\\frac{6}{x}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}-2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\frac{6}{x}$ and $\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(4+\\frac{6}{x}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}-2)} \\\\  & \\to \\frac{4+0+0}{0-2} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the horizontal asymptote is  $y=-2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088221 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "653503b2-4631-561e-af5e-847bcbdfe8e2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Horizontal Asymptote When the Degree of the Numerator is Greater"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8cd3e90b-21d4-5c39-a74f-a8b3dc60ad75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086258} {:answer/id #uuid "91352696-c929-59f2-8b70-c45491cf1e8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086259} {:answer/id #uuid "a74c08c2-9793-54d0-941b-4da5dd3f49bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y =-\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086260} {:answer/id #uuid "ad6056e4-f995-52ac-87a7-70ea33cd15d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y =-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086261} {:answer/id #uuid "da384d83-dedc-5559-bac4-9ec73e540903" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No horizontal asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086262}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "da384d83-dedc-5559-bac4-9ec73e540903" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No horizontal asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086262} :answer-field/id #uuid "80769bb5-2099-5b9f-ac7f-e2bd07c98605" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086257}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0e542b04-fff9-5d2f-9fc8-db21225d104b" :question/math-academy-id "q-6993" :question/problem "Determine the horizontal asymptote of $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{3}+3}{4{x}^{2}-6x}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is ${x}^{2}.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by ${x}^{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{3}+3}{4{x}^{2}-6x} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{4{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{6x}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}})}{(4-\\frac{6}{x})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}$ and $\\frac{6}{x}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(x+\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}})}{(4-\\frac{6}{x})} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{x+0}{4-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is not a constant, which means that $y=f(x)$ does not have a horizontal asymptote."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339769 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6f75252e-bf7d-59ed-bb16-6a2b65a97863" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Horizontal Asymptote When the Degree of the Denominator is Greater"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c18b10d1-12dd-52c3-abca-ae54e1b47fc1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No horizontal asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339771} {:answer/id #uuid "c6836e8d-4412-523c-b1ee-982f2f6e7c52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{3}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339772} {:answer/id #uuid "611aa1e6-0112-5db6-bff9-fb1ad800c6f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339773} {:answer/id #uuid "94cc6206-ac4d-5725-82ff-1ba17f185a68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339774} {:answer/id #uuid "e4502b38-58f9-53a0-98a8-23c423553c42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339775}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "611aa1e6-0112-5db6-bff9-fb1ad800c6f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339773} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b30c470-44d7-5081-aca5-53528b72c34d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339770}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6238a11d-56f3-5197-a3d9-533c6774d65f" :question/math-academy-id "q-6998" :question/problem "Determine the horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x)=\\frac{8-3{x}^{3}}{1-16{x}^{4}}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is ${x}^{4}.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by ${x}^{4},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{8-3{x}^{3}}{1-16{x}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{8}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{3{x}^{3}}{{x}^{4}})}{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{16{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{8}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{3}{x})}{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}-16)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\frac{8}{{x}^{4}},\\frac{3}{x}$ and  $\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}$  become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(\\frac{8}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{3}{x})}{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}-16)} \\\\  & \\to \\frac{0-0}{0-16} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the horizontal asymptote is $y=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339776 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "653503b2-4631-561e-af5e-847bcbdfe8e2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Horizontal Asymptote When the Degree of the Numerator is Greater"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e9fb6b11-5a63-5e40-8e45-2e050a63e959" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No horizontal asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339778} {:answer/id #uuid "134c2ddc-30e7-52ff-b31e-4810ecaa1efd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339779} {:answer/id #uuid "3fffd3ff-b886-5a06-b9ba-d88401ed5c73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{7}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339780} {:answer/id #uuid "45857523-5a83-508b-98cc-ffde7368f930" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339781} {:answer/id #uuid "1022a364-3776-584c-94d9-993561f7365d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339782}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e9fb6b11-5a63-5e40-8e45-2e050a63e959" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No horizontal asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339778} :answer-field/id #uuid "3c6a6827-37f6-5cf6-a794-7a448c22e51e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339777}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fbaacded-fa5e-53e5-bb47-913c990f1827" :question/math-academy-id "q-7005" :question/problem "Determine the horizontal asymptote of $f(x)=\\frac{6-3x+4{x}^{2}}{12x-21}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is $x.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{6-3x+4{x}^{2}}{12x-21} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{6}{x}-\\frac{3x}{x}+\\frac{4{x}^{2}}{x})}{(\\frac{12x}{x}-\\frac{21}{x})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{6}{x}-3+4x)}{(12-\\frac{21}{x})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\frac{6}{x}$ and $\\frac{21}{x}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(\\frac{6}{x}-3+4x)}{(12-\\frac{21}{x})} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{0-3+4x}{12-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{4x-3}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is not a constant, which means that $y=f(x)$ does not have a horizontal asymptote."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088224 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6f75252e-bf7d-59ed-bb16-6a2b65a97863" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Horizontal Asymptote When the Degree of the Denominator is Greater"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d76c4365-db11-56f5-a4ba-6fadf75e7778" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\frac{2}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086276} {:answer/id #uuid "ea5bf088-3903-5838-a423-c67d403f6056" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y =-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086277} {:answer/id #uuid "f9824ec8-13bd-5033-a60f-f8542009a779" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086278} {:answer/id #uuid "11834379-2b4d-5fa4-bab1-e740d43a1a04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086279} {:answer/id #uuid "37385837-155f-54bc-8166-d06d3703aa95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y =-\\frac{1}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086280}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "11834379-2b4d-5fa4-bab1-e740d43a1a04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086279} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab05ef2a-9c6a-50f1-b48d-275148d454bf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086275}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c0349865-9d9c-56c6-b862-8e79d0d94cb7" :question/math-academy-id "q-7006" :question/problem "Determine the horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x)=\\frac{2x+1}{9x+8{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is ${x}^{3}.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by ${x}^{3},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2x+1}{9x+8{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{2x}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{9x}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{8{x}^{2}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{9}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{8}{x}-1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}},\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}},\\frac{9}{{x}^{2}}$ and $\\frac{8}{x}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{9}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{8}{x}-1)} \\\\  & \\to \\frac{0+0}{0+0-1} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the horizontal asymptote is $y=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339783 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "653503b2-4631-561e-af5e-847bcbdfe8e2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Horizontal Asymptote When the Degree of the Numerator is Greater"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b50ddb3f-b8b8-50ae-aaae-a9cf0c338a82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339785} {:answer/id #uuid "87a99ee2-6993-54a2-815b-2add5019df67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339786} {:answer/id #uuid "f25d8501-57eb-53cb-b741-a728d66ca2bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No horizontal asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339787} {:answer/id #uuid "d3a2c4c6-6ade-5677-bab5-393c685a78e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339788} {:answer/id #uuid "cbc30b36-6a79-5afc-bab5-ee81e205e754" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339789}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f25d8501-57eb-53cb-b741-a728d66ca2bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No horizontal asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339787} :answer-field/id #uuid "e329da5d-bd84-5401-8353-d6606dbeb32d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339784}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "225d5964-b5f6-5f9c-9606-7856be911138" :question/math-academy-id "q-81202" :question/problem "Determine the horizontal asymptote of $f(x)=\\frac{3+4{x}^{2}-2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}-x}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is ${x}^{2}.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by ${x}^{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{3+4{x}^{2}-2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}-x} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{4{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}+4-2x)}{(1-\\frac{1}{x})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}$ and $\\frac{1}{x}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}+4-2x)}{(1-\\frac{1}{x})} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{0+4-2x}{1-0} \\\\  & =4-2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is not a constant, which means that $y=f(x)$ does not have a horizontal asymptote."}]}
